WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.259
One of the best things about being a fan of
Jason Statham is that he instantly improves

00:00:04.259 --> 00:00:06.670
any movie he's in, good, bad, or somewhere
in-between.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:11.299
The magnetic British action star has been
cracking skulls and delivering delightfully

00:00:11.299 --> 00:00:13.669
hammy lines for over two decades.

00:00:13.669 --> 00:00:17.840
Whether he's a snarling villain or a hardened
hero, Statham has turned in dozens of extremely

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.939
enjoyable performances since he was first
introduced as part of Guy Ritchie's repertory

00:00:21.939 --> 00:00:24.220
of British toughs in the late nineties.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:27.930
If you're even a casual enjoyer of Statham's
work, then you've probably seen him in The

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:31.929
Transporter and Crank, and also as part of
the impressive ensembles in the Expendables

00:00:31.929 --> 00:00:33.450
and Fast &amp; Furious franchises.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.690
You've probably even caught his surprisingly
effective comedic work in movies like Spy

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:38.690
and Gnomeo &amp; Juliet.

00:00:38.690 --> 00:00:43.460
But there are some movies in the actor's oeuvre
that only hardcore stans of Statham are likely

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:44.478
to have seen.

00:00:44.478 --> 00:00:50.294
Enter one of Netflix's current top 10 most
watched movies: the 2013 action movie Homefront.

00:00:50.294 --> 00:00:54.289
There's a good chance the name isn't ringing
any bells, as despite some obvious draws,

00:00:54.289 --> 00:00:58.519
like James Franco playing a character named
Gator, Winona Ryder as a hardened criminal,

00:00:58.519 --> 00:01:01.630
and of course, Statham himself, the movie
never made much of a splash.

00:01:04.939 --> 00:01:07.950
"Whatever you're thinking… rethink it."

00:01:08.904 --> 00:01:12.830
But as so many films have in the past, it's
getting another life thanks to Netflix.

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:16.270
Here's what you need to know about this overlooked
Jason Statham action thriller.

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:21.450
After a violent drug raid gone bad and the
loss of his wife, DEA Agent Phil Broker decides

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:25.626
to take his daughter, Maddy and retire in
what he believes will be a quiet Louisiana parish.

00:01:25.626 --> 00:01:29.390
Unfortunately, it's not long before Broker
learns that not all of his new neighbors are

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:31.619
interested in rolling out the welcome wagon.

00:01:31.619 --> 00:01:35.259
After an altercation with some schoolyard
bullies, Broker and Maddy land on the radar

00:01:35.259 --> 00:01:40.220
of local meth kingpin Gator, his desperately
addicted sister, Cassie, and his vicious right-hand

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:41.220
woman, Sheryl.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:45.170
What starts as a game of intimidation quickly
escalates when Gator learns that Broker isn't

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:49.700
just some new guy in town, but a retired DEA
agent, and Broker decides to put his old job

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:52.550
skills to work to try and dismantle Gator's
operation.

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:55.689
Both sides find themselves entrenched in a
violent back and forth.

00:01:55.689 --> 00:01:59.549
Broker may be outnumbered, but when Gator
and Sheryl orchestrate the kidnapping of Maddy,

00:01:59.549 --> 00:02:03.049
he taps into a well of fury that the small
town criminals couldn't have understood the depths of.

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:07.628
Unfortunately for them, now that they've crossed
the line, there's no going back.

00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:15.213
"Here's what you need to know, Broker -- you
have got my attention, son."

00:02:17.339 --> 00:02:20.840
Statham has rarely met a group of fictional
malicious criminals who he hasn't wanted to

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.410
kick the dirt out of, but when it comes to
Homefront, there was another big pull that

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:25.560
helped bring him on board.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:30.250
The movie was written by Statham's Expendables
co-star and Oscar nominated screenwriter Sylvester

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:31.250
Stallone.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:32.480
As Statham told Den of Geek,

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:33.970
"He's one of the great writers.

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:36.740
I think people forget how many great films
he's written.

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:41.877
You know, he wrote Cliffhanger, he wrote all
of the Rockys, all of the Rambos, all of the Expendables films.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.371
It's like a library of mega-success."

00:02:44.371 --> 00:02:48.050
In fact, when Stallone first wrote the script
-- which is based on a novel of the same name

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:50.717
by Chuck Logan -- he intended to star in it
himself.

00:02:50.717 --> 00:02:54.500
When it came time to actually make the movie,
however, the legendary action star tapped

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:55.870
Statham to take his place.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:59.530
While speaking with Den of Geek, Statham said
that getting the call was, quote, "one of

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:04.169
the biggest privileges you can get… a hand-delivered
script for something [Stallone] wrote for himself."

00:03:04.358 --> 00:03:08.200
Critics also found plenty to enjoy in the
movie, which was summed up by many as simple

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:09.340
but satisfying.

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:11.660
Noah Berlatsky, writing for The Atlantic,
said that

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.319
"As a big dumb action movie, it works pretty
well.

00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:17.630
[...] Overall, Homefront delivers the enjoyment
you'd expect from a shoot-‘em-up."

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:21.320
Manohla Dargis of The New York Times summed
up the experience of watching Homefront by

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:23.546
calling it "indefensibly enjoyable."

00:03:23.546 --> 00:03:25.602
Andy Lea of the Daily Star said

00:03:25.602 --> 00:03:28.070
"Nobody can write this stuff quite like Stallone.

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:31.780
And nobody from this over-drama schooled,
over-Botoxed generation of movie stars can

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:33.099
play it like Statham."

00:03:33.099 --> 00:03:36.520
You can judge for yourself by checking out
Homefront on Netflix.

